<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Compliance Rates | FORRT - Framework for Open and Reproducible Research Training</title><link>https://staging.forrt.org/tag/compliance-rates/</link><atom:link href="https://staging.forrt.org/tag/compliance-rates/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><description>Compliance Rates</description><generator>Source Themes Academic (https://sourcethemes.com/academic/)</generator><language>en-us</language><copyright>© 2025 - FORRT > Framework for Open and Reproducible Research Training</copyright><lastBuildDate>Sun, 09 Oct 2016 00:00:00 +0000</lastBuildDate><image><url>https://staging.forrt.org/img/FORRT_banner.svg</url><title>Compliance Rates</title><link>https://staging.forrt.org/tag/compliance-rates/</link></image><item><title>KRAWCZYK: Good News/Bad News on Who Gets the Data</title><link>https://staging.forrt.org/replication-hub/blog/krawczyk-good-news-bad-news-on-who-gets-the-data/</link><pubDate>Sun, 09 Oct 2016 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://staging.forrt.org/replication-hub/blog/krawczyk-good-news-bad-news-on-who-gets-the-data/</guid><description>&lt;h6 id="providing-access-to-the-data-is-a-prerequisite-for-replication-of-empirical-analysis-unfortunately-this-access-is-not-always-granted-to-everyone-see-herehttpjournalsplosorgplosonearticleid101371journalpone0021101-and-herehttpwwwncbinlmnihgovpmcarticlespmc4533904-there-is-evidence-that-some-of-this-may-be-due-to-concerns-about-requestors-qualifications-see-herehttpsbooksgooglechbookshlenlridijuraaaayaajoifndpgpr1dqfienbergandmartin1985otsbg1rhqktvksigxxkl267n6uzbmbqc1x3o8asc7pqvonepageqfienberg20and20martin201985ffalse">Providing access to the data is a prerequisite for replication of empirical analysis. Unfortunately, this access is not always granted to everyone (see &lt;em>&lt;strong>
&lt;a href="http://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0021101" target="_blank" rel="noopener">here&lt;/a>&lt;/strong>&lt;/em>, and &lt;em>&lt;strong>
&lt;a href="http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4533904/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">here&lt;/a>&lt;/strong>&lt;/em>).  There is evidence that some of this may be due to concerns about requestors’ qualifications (see &lt;em>&lt;strong>
&lt;a href="https://books.google.ch/books?hl=en&amp;amp;lr=&amp;amp;id=ijUrAAAAYAAJ&amp;amp;oi=fnd&amp;amp;pg=PR1&amp;amp;dq=Fienberg&amp;#43;and&amp;#43;Martin&amp;#43;1985&amp;amp;ots=bG1rHqkTvk&amp;amp;sig=xxkl267n6UZbMBQc1X3o8ASc7PQ#v=onepage&amp;amp;q=Fienberg%20and%20Martin%201985&amp;amp;f=false" target="_blank" rel="noopener">here&lt;/a>&lt;/strong>&lt;/em>).&lt;/h6>
&lt;h6 id="in-two-recent-papers-we-investigated-how-willingness-to-share-depended-on-the-identity-of-the-requestor-in-a-paper-entitled-unavailable-upon-request-field-experiment-on-researchers-willingness-to-share-supplementary-materialshttpswwwncbinlmnihgovpubmed22686633-ernesto-reuben-and-i-found-that-only-44-of-economists-were-willing-to-share-supplementary-research-materials-of-a-published-study-they-had-promised-to-send-upon-request-this-number-was-slightly-higher-if-the-request-came-from-a-high-prestigious-university-rather-than-one-that-was-less-prestigious">In two recent papers, we investigated how willingness to share depended on the identity of the requestor. In a paper entitled, &lt;strong>
&lt;a href="https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/22686633" target="_blank" rel="noopener">&lt;em>“(Un)available upon request: field experiment on researchers’ willingness to share supplementary materials&lt;/em>&lt;/a>&lt;/strong>&lt;em>”&lt;/em>,  Ernesto Reuben and I  found that only 44% of economists were willing to share supplementary research materials of a published study they had promised to send “upon request”.  This number was slightly higher if the request came from a high prestigious university rather than one that was less prestigious.&lt;/h6>
&lt;h6 id="in-gender-beauty-and-support-networks-in-academia-evidence-from-a-field-experimenthttpeconpapersrepecorgpaperwarwpaper2015-43htm-study-1-magdalena-smyk-and-i-asked-experimental-economists-to-share-data-they-used-in-their-published-paper-the-focus-in-this-experiment-was-the-role-of-gender-half-the-requests-were-sent-by-a-female-student-the-other-half-by-a-male-overall-compliance-rate-was-347-with-no-gender-effect">In “&lt;strong>
&lt;a href="http://econpapers.repec.org/paper/warwpaper/2015-43.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">&lt;em>Gender, beauty and support networks in academia: evidence from a field experiment&lt;/em>&lt;/a>&lt;/strong> (Study 1)”, Magdalena Smyk and I asked experimental economists to share data they used in their published paper. The focus in this experiment was the role of gender: half the requests were sent by a female student, the other half by a male. Overall compliance rate was 34.7%, with no gender effect.&lt;/h6>
&lt;h6 id="the-fact-that-requestors-identity-matters-little-is-good-news-of-course-as-it-suggests-a-roughly-level-playing-field-however-while-compliance-rates-in-economics-are-higher-than-many-other-disciplines-see-herehttpsorgtheorywordpresscom20150811sociologists-need-to-be-better-at-replication-a-guest-post-by-cristobal-youngcomment-140734-and-herehttpjournalsplosorgplosonearticleid101371journalpone0007078-and-herehttpwwwcellcomcurrent-biologyabstracts0960-98221301400-0-there-is-still-much-room-for-improvement">The fact that requestor’s identity matters little is good news of course, as it suggests a roughly level playing field. However, while compliance rates in economics are higher than many other disciplines (see &lt;em>&lt;strong>
&lt;a href="https://orgtheory.wordpress.com/2015/08/11/sociologists-need-to-be-better-at-replication-a-guest-post-by-cristobal-young/#comment-140734" target="_blank" rel="noopener">here&lt;/a>&lt;/strong>&lt;/em> and &lt;em>&lt;strong>
&lt;a href="http://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0007078" target="_blank" rel="noopener">here&lt;/a>&lt;/strong>&lt;/em> and &lt;em>&lt;strong>
&lt;a href="http://www.cell.com/current-biology/abstract/S0960-9822%2813%2901400-0" target="_blank" rel="noopener">here&lt;/a>&lt;/strong>&lt;/em>), there is still much room for improvement.&lt;/h6>
&lt;h6 id="michal-krawczyk-is-an-assistant-professor-of-economic-sciences-at-the-university-of-warsaw-poland">&lt;em>Michal Krawczyk is an Assistant Professor of Economic Sciences at the University of Warsaw, Poland.&lt;/em>&lt;/h6>
&lt;h6 id="references">REFERENCES&lt;/h6>
&lt;h6 id="krawczyk-m--reuben-e-2012-un-available-upon-request-field-experiment-on-researchers-willingness-to-share-supplementary-materialsaccountability-in-research-193-175-186">Krawczyk, M., &amp;amp; Reuben, E. (2012). (Un) Available upon Request: Field Experiment on Researchers’ Willingness to Share Supplementary Materials.Accountability in research, 19(3), 175-186.&lt;/h6>
